1
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:03,370
My old house?

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:05,580
Did I really travel back in time?

3
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:08,700
(To have other things
to think about when I die,)

4
00:00:09,210 --> 00:00:11,490
this Jiraphat will become a good boy.

5
00:00:16,030 --> 00:00:17,540
You can’t smoke in school.

6
00:00:17,660 --> 00:00:18,500
You jackass!

7
00:00:18,930 --> 00:00:20,400
Ji, catch this.

8
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:27,620
Jiraphat.

9
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:30,400
Why are you staring at me like that?

10
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:33,490
Wait. What date and year is it?

11
00:00:33,510 --> 00:00:34,900
Why are you asking?

12
00:00:35,410 --> 00:00:37,620
May 31, 2010.

13
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:19,370
NARONGRIT BOXING GYM

14
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:34,060
Are you following me?

15
00:02:36,070 --> 00:02:37,820
No, I’m not. Get over yourself.

16
00:02:37,960 --> 00:02:39,100
You go to the boxing gym?

17
00:02:40,730 --> 00:02:41,980
Yes, this is my house.

18
00:02:43,640 --> 00:02:45,540
But the owner is related to Mr. Narong.

19
00:02:45,810 --> 00:02:46,500
Mmm.

20
00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:48,400
So you and Mr. Narong are…

21
00:02:48,710 --> 00:02:49,800
He’s my uncle.

22
00:02:52,920 --> 00:02:54,700
But your family names are different.

23
00:02:55,760 --> 00:02:58,660
He’s from my mom’s side,
so we have different names.

24
00:02:59,780 --> 00:03:01,060
What’s with these bruises on your body?

25
00:03:01,250 --> 00:03:02,200
From boxing training.

26
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:05,950
You think I always get into fist fights
like people say?

27
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:08,840
So who knows about this?

28
00:03:08,870 --> 00:03:11,140
Apart from you, just two people.

29
00:03:12,260 --> 00:03:13,100
-Lita.
-Lita.

30
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:15,340
And who’s the other one?

31
00:03:16,340 --> 00:03:17,260
The president.

32
00:03:18,410 --> 00:03:19,300
The president?

33
00:03:19,580 --> 00:03:20,730
Yeah, why?

34
00:03:22,630 --> 00:03:24,400
Nothing. I should go.

35
00:03:27,640 --> 00:03:29,770
NARONGRIT BOXING GYM

36
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:35,700
I’m home, mom.

37
00:03:36,090 --> 00:03:38,380
You’re back? How was your day?

38
00:03:42,580 --> 00:03:44,100
I’ve made a new friend today.

39
00:03:44,470 --> 00:03:45,310
His name’s Kong.

40
00:03:45,350 --> 00:03:47,380
He’s related to the owner
of the boxing gym around the corner.

41
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:50,040
Why don’t you invite him here sometime?

42
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:51,500
Ty, too.

43
00:03:53,600 --> 00:03:54,700
Okay, I will.

44
00:04:07,990 --> 00:04:10,400
Ked, Pim,
what is everyone freaking out about?

45
00:04:10,990 --> 00:04:13,300
It’s Ms. Suda’s homework, of course.

46
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:16,800
Seriously, who would finish it in time?

47
00:04:17,290 --> 00:04:18,430
She barely taught us anything.

48
00:04:18,470 --> 00:04:20,050
I could only do five items.

49
00:04:20,660 --> 00:04:23,060
Yeah, I could only do five of them, too.

50
00:04:23,230 --> 00:04:24,690
What? What do you mean five?

51
00:04:24,710 --> 00:04:25,450
Eh?

52
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:26,790
You’ve just copied mine.

53
00:04:26,820 --> 00:04:28,310
Well, if I hadn’t left
the school club so late,

54
00:04:28,340 --> 00:04:30,200
I would have finished five items, too.

55
00:04:30,890 --> 00:04:32,160
What about you, Ji?

56
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:33,660
Do you want to copy mine?

57
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:36,180
No, thanks. I finished it.

58
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:38,000
-Huh?
-Huh?

59
00:04:38,710 --> 00:04:39,700
You finished?

60
00:04:39,850 --> 00:04:40,380
Mmm.

61
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:41,700
All ten items?

62
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:43,340
Mmm.

63
00:04:51,470 --> 00:04:53,540
Guys, Ji’s finished his homework.

64
00:05:00,610 --> 00:05:02,540
You could do all of them?

65
00:05:03,020 --> 00:05:06,580
(Of course, I could. I studied a program
that requires math scores.)

66
00:05:06,620 --> 00:05:10,420
(I work in finance, and I’ve studied
all this material before.)

67
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:12,950
I did what I could, but I’m not so sure.

68
00:05:17,870 --> 00:05:19,810
-Give it to me when you’re finished, okay?
-Yeah.

69
00:05:19,950 --> 00:05:20,700
You’re late.

70
00:05:20,990 --> 00:05:23,340
My dad forgot something
so we had to turn back.

71
00:05:23,690 --> 00:05:26,340
And it’s been raining so heavily.
I’m all soaked.

72
00:05:30,100 --> 00:05:32,700
What is everyone doing?

73
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:34,660
Ms. Suda’s ten-item homework.

74
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:36,500
Have you finished it?

75
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:39,900
Who’s Suda?

76
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:46,090
Everyone,

77
00:05:46,350 --> 00:05:49,260
I have two important announcements
to make today.

78
00:05:49,710 --> 00:05:54,100
First, there’s been a small change
to this year’s field trip.

79
00:05:54,640 --> 00:05:56,100
We’re going to…

80
00:05:58,960 --> 00:06:02,700
Uh, could everyone please listen to me?

81
00:06:04,210 --> 00:06:05,500
Listen up, guys.

82
00:06:06,270 --> 00:06:08,700
I just need
three minutes of your time, okay?

83
00:06:09,870 --> 00:06:14,810
Okay, first, there’s been a small change
to this year’s field trip.

84
00:06:14,970 --> 00:06:16,380
Instead of a day trip,

85
00:06:16,420 --> 00:06:18,690
we’ll be staying overnight
for two days and one night.

86
00:06:18,730 --> 00:06:20,620
This is because
we’ve received support from…

87
00:06:20,660 --> 00:06:22,390
Uh, okay.

88
00:06:22,430 --> 00:06:25,900
Let’s go over the details
at the meeting instead.

89
00:06:26,340 --> 00:06:28,500
Now, for the second announcement,

90
00:06:28,910 --> 00:06:30,980
the upcoming Wai Khru ceremony.

91
00:06:31,280 --> 00:06:34,980
Each class must select five students
to make the ceremonial offering trays.

92
00:06:35,180 --> 00:06:37,100
Our Class 7 already has three volunteers:

93
00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:40,100
Kedsarin, Busaba, and Phappim.

94
00:06:40,220 --> 00:06:43,800
I need two more.
Anyone willing to volunteer?

95
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:50,340
Oh, wow! We have so many volunteers.

96
00:06:50,640 --> 00:06:53,000
The offering trays
don’t have to be that beautiful.

97
00:06:53,020 --> 00:06:54,500
Just make sure you finish them.

98
00:06:54,540 --> 00:06:56,800
As for the materials,
since Busaba’s family runs a flower shop…

99
00:06:56,830 --> 00:06:59,380
-Hey.
-Sir, Jiraphat wants to do it.

100
00:06:59,900 --> 00:07:00,740
Really, Jiraphat?

101
00:07:05,050 --> 00:07:06,740
-I guess, sir.
-Terrific.

102
00:07:06,940 --> 00:07:11,660
Kedsarin, can you please add
Jiraphat and Tyler to the list?

103
00:07:12,100 --> 00:07:14,300
Huh? Me, too?!

104
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:16,420
Why do I have to do it?

105
00:07:16,580 --> 00:07:18,660
Well, didn’t you raise your hand?

106
00:07:18,980 --> 00:07:20,990
He did. Tyler did raise his hand.

107
00:07:21,120 --> 00:07:22,290
Okay, we’ve got enough people.

108
00:07:22,310 --> 00:07:23,510
Let’s give them a hand.

109
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:25,620
Thank you.

110
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:33,940
Attention, students.

111
00:07:34,450 --> 00:07:36,700
Good morning.

112
00:07:37,870 --> 00:07:38,800
Now let’s see.

113
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:42,340
How well have you all prepared
for my class?

114
00:07:42,750 --> 00:07:45,900
I’ll randomly call on students
to do the homework I assigned.

115
00:07:46,180 --> 00:07:50,480
(No wonder I used to skip
almost every math class in my past life.)

116
00:07:50,570 --> 00:07:52,260
Jiraphat, number 7.

117
00:07:52,850 --> 00:07:54,200
Let me see your notebook.

118
00:08:08,500 --> 00:08:10,140
You did all this yourself, Jiraphat?

119
00:08:11,340 --> 00:08:12,060
Yes, ma’am.

120
00:08:12,330 --> 00:08:13,940
I haven’t taught these 10 problems yet.

121
00:08:13,980 --> 00:08:15,340
How did you get them all right?

122
00:08:16,170 --> 00:08:18,140
Did you copy the answers
from another class?

123
00:08:19,080 --> 00:08:20,260
No, I didn’t.

124
00:08:26,180 --> 00:08:29,300
Then show me
how you solve problem number 10.

125
00:09:08,760 --> 00:09:10,500
I don’t believe you can do this yourself.

126
00:09:11,890 --> 00:09:13,420
Did you copy it from someone else?

127
00:09:16,590 --> 00:09:18,270
Who would I copy from?

128
00:09:18,450 --> 00:09:21,340
Then who can vouch for you, huh?

129
00:09:26,450 --> 00:09:27,940
Ji did it himself, ma’am.

130
00:09:28,590 --> 00:09:29,940
I copied from him.

131
00:09:32,980 --> 00:09:34,740
I copied from Jiraphat, too.

132
00:09:36,070 --> 00:09:36,980
Me, too.

133
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:39,400
Jiraphat did it all by himself.

134
00:09:39,630 --> 00:09:40,450
I couldn’t do it.

135
00:09:40,590 --> 00:09:42,300
-Me, neither.
-Me, neither.

136
00:09:42,750 --> 00:09:43,540
Me, neither.

137
00:09:44,410 --> 00:09:45,200
Me, neither.

138
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:50,660
Since you all cheated,

139
00:09:50,700 --> 00:09:52,500
there’s no point in me
teaching you anymore.

140
00:10:03,190 --> 00:10:04,600
Don’t mind her, Ji.

141
00:10:04,940 --> 00:10:07,740
I think she wants to finish
her own work anyway.

142
00:10:08,020 --> 00:10:10,980
If it weren’t you, she’d probably
pick on another student anyway.

143
00:10:13,300 --> 00:10:15,380
Right. The audacity.

144
00:10:15,690 --> 00:10:18,100
She didn’t teach us anything.
Who could have done it?

145
00:10:20,700 --> 00:10:24,860
Ji, you looked so cool
while solving that problem.

146
00:10:27,640 --> 00:10:30,260
Why don’t you just teach us
how to solve the problems?

147
00:10:30,970 --> 00:10:35,000
But if Ms. Suda finds out,
won’t she be mad at us?

148
00:10:35,290 --> 00:10:37,690
How can a teacher
you only see a few times a week

149
00:10:37,950 --> 00:10:40,700
compare to classmates
you spend the whole year with?

150
00:10:45,780 --> 00:10:48,820
Okay, let’s start with the basics, then.

151
00:10:49,260 --> 00:10:52,200
You can refer to your textbooks as we go.

152
00:10:52,540 --> 00:10:55,700
(If I hadn’t taken everyone for granted
13 years ago,)

153
00:10:55,820 --> 00:10:58,300
(I might not be living
with regrets at 30.)

154
00:10:58,850 --> 00:11:02,370
(No one should look down on Class 5/7.)

155
00:11:02,610 --> 00:11:03,610
(Not even me.)

156
00:11:07,520 --> 00:11:10,040
ATIWITCH RATTANAWATTHANA,
CLASS PRESIDENT

157
00:11:10,080 --> 00:11:11,800
What should we do about Ms. Suda?

158
00:11:12,020 --> 00:11:13,520
If this keeps up,

159
00:11:13,560 --> 00:11:16,000
the Science-Math and Arts-Language classes
are bound to fight.

160
00:11:16,200 --> 00:11:18,540
What’s the problem?
She teaches pretty well.

161
00:11:18,820 --> 00:11:21,700
She did a great job teaching matrices.

162
00:11:21,800 --> 00:11:25,220
You only say that because she favors you.

163
00:11:25,390 --> 00:11:28,820
Today, she walked out of my class
before it was even half over.

164
00:11:29,070 --> 00:11:33,780
Luckily, Jiraphat
was able to teach us instead.

165
00:11:38,090 --> 00:11:40,800
Since we can’t agree,
what should we do, President?

166
00:11:50,040 --> 00:11:51,510
SCHOOL CO-OP

167
00:11:51,550 --> 00:11:54,660
Oh, you saved us today, Ji.

168
00:11:55,710 --> 00:11:57,830
That’s right. I’m still shocked.

169
00:11:58,310 --> 00:12:01,020
In grade 10, you didn’t pay
this much attention in class.

170
00:12:01,370 --> 00:12:06,620
What turned that unbothered Jiraphat
into Jiraphat The Good Boy?

171
00:12:07,090 --> 00:12:08,650
Would you stop calling me that?

172
00:12:08,670 --> 00:12:09,250
Oh.

173
00:12:09,320 --> 00:12:11,300
Why, Ji? I think it’s kind of cute.

174
00:12:11,740 --> 00:12:12,820
Jiraphat The Good Boy.

175
00:12:13,150 --> 00:12:14,260
Tyler The Good Boy.

176
00:12:17,420 --> 00:12:18,820
Kongpob The Good Boy!

177
00:12:22,930 --> 00:12:24,860
-Did you spill your rice?
-Just one.

178
00:12:25,470 --> 00:12:27,850
Where is it now? Every rice counts.

179
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:29,240
Come and sit, my friend, Kong.

180
00:12:33,750 --> 00:12:36,000
That’s it for today.
Class dismissed, everyone.

181
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:38,180
Thank you, ma’am.

182
00:12:40,860 --> 00:12:43,100
Hey, that evaluation form
you asked me to make,

183
00:12:43,570 --> 00:12:44,500
I’ve finished it.

184
00:12:45,870 --> 00:12:48,300
And I’ve talked to some of the students.

185
00:12:48,600 --> 00:12:51,100
We should have enough evidence
to present to the school.

186
00:12:51,780 --> 00:12:54,900
But why did you ask me
to conduct the interviews?

187
00:12:55,150 --> 00:12:56,820
I’m not a journalist.

188
00:12:58,220 --> 00:13:00,700
Come on. Think of it as aearly internship.

189
00:13:01,510 --> 00:13:02,250
What?

190
00:13:02,800 --> 00:13:03,900
How would you know about that?

191
00:13:11,650 --> 00:13:13,300
Hey, you’re leaving?

192
00:13:13,930 --> 00:13:15,600
-Mmm.
-Why leave so early today?

193
00:13:16,600 --> 00:13:18,400
I have to talk to my dad about something.

194
00:13:18,790 --> 00:13:19,650
See you later.

195
00:13:27,550 --> 00:13:29,000
I signed up to make the offering tray.

196
00:13:30,120 --> 00:13:32,300
So you can go home with us?

197
00:13:32,600 --> 00:13:33,600
Of course not.

198
00:13:34,090 --> 00:13:35,540
Don't act nonchalant.

199
00:13:36,050 --> 00:13:38,540
It's to celebrate
our unbreakable friendship…

200
00:13:38,890 --> 00:13:41,300
Uncle, can I have a Power Rangers pancake?

201
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:42,300
I want one, too.

202
00:13:42,330 --> 00:13:43,660
What? I don’t know what that is!

203
00:13:46,330 --> 00:13:49,400
Then I’ll have three custard pancakes.

204
00:13:50,020 --> 00:13:51,550
And minced pork pancakes, too.

205
00:13:51,580 --> 00:13:52,900
That I can do.

206
00:14:11,940 --> 00:14:12,900
I’m home.

207
00:14:14,270 --> 00:14:16,200
You’re back? How was school?

208
00:14:16,480 --> 00:14:17,460
It was fine.

209
00:14:20,030 --> 00:14:22,200
Mom, well…

210
00:14:25,170 --> 00:14:25,950
Come in.

211
00:14:28,530 --> 00:14:29,260
Good evening.

212
00:14:32,370 --> 00:14:33,400
This is Tyler.

213
00:14:34,470 --> 00:14:36,300
And this is Kongpob.

214
00:14:36,690 --> 00:14:39,900
Oh, my. Why didn’t you tell me
you invited your friends home?

215
00:14:40,070 --> 00:14:41,800
I would have prepared more food.

216
00:14:42,160 --> 00:14:45,000
That’s alright, mom.
They’re only here for a moment.

217
00:14:45,890 --> 00:14:47,830
My customer’s coming
to pick up the desserts.

218
00:14:47,850 --> 00:14:49,600
Can you give them these?

219
00:14:49,990 --> 00:14:50,940
Where are you going?

220
00:14:51,130 --> 00:14:52,940
I’ll go make more food.

221
00:14:53,150 --> 00:14:54,580
Make yourselves at home.

222
00:14:54,880 --> 00:14:55,680
-Thank you.
-Thank you.

223
00:14:55,710 --> 00:14:56,730
Do you need a hand?

224
00:14:56,960 --> 00:14:58,820
It’s okay. You hang out with your friends.

225
00:15:00,760 --> 00:15:02,500
Have you ever tried Thong Yot?

226
00:15:02,830 --> 00:15:05,100
Are we disturbing your mom?

227
00:15:05,930 --> 00:15:08,700
No, she’s just surprised.

228
00:15:09,040 --> 00:15:10,670
‘Cause I’ve never
brought any friends home.

229
00:15:14,130 --> 00:15:18,340
Oh, hi, kitty. You’re so cute.

230
00:15:18,440 --> 00:15:19,460
Kitty, kitty, kitty.

231
00:15:20,240 --> 00:15:21,460
Kitty, kitty, kitty.

232
00:15:26,840 --> 00:15:29,900
Here, my mom made some hot cocoa.

233
00:15:30,130 --> 00:15:31,580
Her special recipe.

234
00:15:32,840 --> 00:15:33,580
Try it.

235
00:15:34,040 --> 00:15:35,000
Thank you.

236
00:15:35,330 --> 00:15:36,700
-Kongpob.
-Here.

237
00:15:43,730 --> 00:15:44,550
Hmm.

238
00:15:45,140 --> 00:15:46,060
So delicious, Ji.

239
00:15:46,990 --> 00:15:48,300
One cup is not enough.

240
00:15:48,420 --> 00:15:50,300
Yeah. Better than what those cafés make.

241
00:15:50,590 --> 00:15:51,330
True.

242
00:15:57,050 --> 00:15:57,800
What?

243
00:15:58,550 --> 00:15:59,460
My butt hurts.

244
00:15:59,970 --> 00:16:00,700
Yeah.

245
00:16:01,600 --> 00:16:06,140
Yeah, I’ve asked my parents many times
to buy couches instead.

246
00:16:08,740 --> 00:16:10,800
Tyler, do you have a couch at home?

247
00:16:11,370 --> 00:16:12,200
Of course, Ji.

248
00:16:12,440 --> 00:16:15,900
I have a huge comfortable couch.

249
00:16:16,230 --> 00:16:17,780
You can even sleep in it.

250
00:16:18,080 --> 00:16:21,000
And it’s not so hard like this one, Ji.

251
00:16:22,050 --> 00:16:22,780
Tyler.

252
00:16:27,030 --> 00:16:28,300
Ji, I have a question.

253
00:16:28,590 --> 00:16:29,200
Hmm?

254
00:16:29,640 --> 00:16:31,220
What's bigger than a whale?

255
00:16:31,350 --> 00:16:32,700
A deep-fried whale.

256
00:16:32,720 --> 00:16:34,550
Then what's bigger than
a deep-fried whale?

257
00:16:34,590 --> 00:16:35,860
The pan used to deep-fry a whale.

258
00:16:35,890 --> 00:16:37,860
Ugh, how about this?

259
00:16:38,170 --> 00:16:39,200
Name a colorful jelly.

260
00:16:39,240 --> 00:16:39,830
Pipo.

261
00:16:39,860 --> 00:16:41,490
Ugh, name a jelly with one color.

262
00:16:41,530 --> 00:16:42,380
One Pipo.

263
00:16:42,410 --> 00:16:43,570
Name a jelly with no color.

264
00:16:43,600 --> 00:16:45,000
No Pipo.

265
00:16:45,600 --> 00:16:46,840
How did you know that, Ji?

266
00:16:46,880 --> 00:16:48,370
You cheated, didn’t you?

267
00:16:48,800 --> 00:16:50,650
Is this what you two usually do?

268
00:16:50,690 --> 00:16:52,780
Here, I’ve got another question.

269
00:16:52,810 --> 00:16:54,500
This one is challenging.

270
00:16:54,880 --> 00:17:01,100
Which part of your body is long,
hard, and hairy?

271
00:17:02,200 --> 00:17:03,100
-Tyler!
-Tyler!

272
00:17:03,240 --> 00:17:05,010
It’s your leg, of course.

273
00:17:05,450 --> 00:17:07,270
That one was hard, right?

274
00:17:07,310 --> 00:17:09,360
No one knew the answer.

275
00:17:09,660 --> 00:17:12,800
Do you even know
that question was a dirty joke?

276
00:17:14,120 --> 00:17:15,420
What? I don’t know.

277
00:17:16,800 --> 00:17:17,550
Yeah.

278
00:17:19,780 --> 00:17:22,060
Hey, Kong, my friend. Come here.

279
00:17:22,480 --> 00:17:23,400
I have a question.

280
00:17:23,840 --> 00:17:25,060
This one is so cool.

281
00:17:27,290 --> 00:17:29,020
The question is

282
00:17:29,340 --> 00:17:35,260
Which part of your body is long,
hard, and hairy?

283
00:17:36,050 --> 00:17:37,000
Hmm.

284
00:17:37,770 --> 00:17:38,780
I don’t know. What?

285
00:17:39,060 --> 00:17:40,780
Your leg, of course.

286
00:17:41,110 --> 00:17:42,460
Ah, right.

287
00:17:42,870 --> 00:17:44,760
See? My question was great, wasn’t it?

288
00:17:44,790 --> 00:17:46,400
Yeah, yeah. Do you have more?

289
00:17:46,430 --> 00:17:47,740
I certainly do.

290
00:17:48,590 --> 00:17:52,450
A billionaire is very superstitious…

291
00:17:52,490 --> 00:17:54,400
We’re here, we’re here.

292
00:17:55,160 --> 00:17:56,900
Let’s get to work, everyone.

293
00:17:57,760 --> 00:17:58,900
-Thank you.
-Thank you.

294
00:17:58,930 --> 00:18:00,820
-Pass that around.
-I wasn’t finished.

295
00:18:00,850 --> 00:18:01,820
Pass that around.

296
00:18:02,820 --> 00:18:04,200
Let’s do this.

297
00:18:04,330 --> 00:18:07,780
Ji, what’s this?

298
00:18:08,190 --> 00:18:10,000
-A crown flower.
-A crown flower.

299
00:18:13,720 --> 00:18:15,100
What about this?

300
00:18:15,910 --> 00:18:17,300
Bermuda grass.

301
00:18:17,800 --> 00:18:18,980
Bermuda grass.

302
00:18:19,730 --> 00:18:23,460
And what are these fruits for?

303
00:18:23,620 --> 00:18:24,460
Can I eat them?

304
00:18:24,530 --> 00:18:26,900
Hey, they’re for the offering trays.

305
00:18:27,290 --> 00:18:28,220
Offering trays?
Mmm.

306
00:18:29,210 --> 00:18:31,300
Mr. Tin said that the trays
don’t have to look beautiful.

307
00:18:31,340 --> 00:18:34,260
So we’re using these fruits as materials.

308
00:18:34,730 --> 00:18:37,550
We can eat them after the ceremony.
No food waste.

309
00:18:37,590 --> 00:18:38,800
Mmm, right, right.

310
00:18:41,050 --> 00:18:42,980
Tyler, what are you chewing?

311
00:18:43,510 --> 00:18:44,260
Popcorn.

312
00:18:45,450 --> 00:18:46,750
This popcorn tastes weird.

313
00:18:46,780 --> 00:18:48,700
It’s bland and stale, too.

314
00:18:49,010 --> 00:18:50,770
Don’t buy this again.

315
00:18:51,190 --> 00:18:52,500
That is not popcorn.

316
00:18:53,150 --> 00:18:54,500
It’s popped rice.

317
00:18:54,850 --> 00:18:56,860
It’s for the decorations.

318
00:18:59,380 --> 00:19:01,780
Eww. Eww, eww, eww.

319
00:19:02,400 --> 00:19:03,650
Disgusting.

320
00:19:03,700 --> 00:19:05,860
That’s disgusting. Why did you eat that?

321
00:19:05,890 --> 00:19:09,220
-It doesn’t taste good at all.
-Yeah, ‘cause it’s not popcorn.

322
00:19:09,560 --> 00:19:12,650
RECORD OF MISCONDUCT

323
00:19:16,560 --> 00:19:18,890
LIST OF STUDENTS ABSENT FROM MATH CLASS
MS. SUDA WIRIYAPHONG

324
00:19:20,320 --> 00:19:23,810
JIRAPHAT JAROEWONG,
GRADE 10, CLASS 7, 10 ABSENCES

325
00:19:26,610 --> 00:19:29,000
President, Ms. Suda wants to see you.

326
00:19:29,480 --> 00:19:33,980
STUDENT COUNCIL

327
00:19:34,110 --> 00:19:37,540
I heard you’re leading the effort to file
a complaint against me.

328
00:19:38,050 --> 00:19:39,950
There must be some misunderstanding.

329
00:19:48,090 --> 00:19:49,300
That is so disappointing.

330
00:19:49,660 --> 00:19:52,000
You used to be such an obedient student.

331
00:19:52,430 --> 00:19:53,980
I wonder what made you this way.

332
00:19:56,480 --> 00:20:00,380
You’ve been hanging around
with the students in Class 7, right?

333
00:20:00,400 --> 00:20:01,200
Ma’am.

334
00:20:06,420 --> 00:20:09,200
You’re the reason I have to do this.

335
00:20:13,740 --> 00:20:14,740
What do you mean?

336
00:20:17,680 --> 00:20:19,740
If you had done your job properly,

337
00:20:20,630 --> 00:20:21,930
treated everyone fairly,

338
00:20:22,620 --> 00:20:24,290
and upheld professional teaching ethics,

339
00:20:25,300 --> 00:20:27,100
we wouldn’t have had to do this.

340
00:20:31,870 --> 00:20:33,340
What do you want me to do?

341
00:20:34,090 --> 00:20:35,700
Apologize to those students?

342
00:20:37,450 --> 00:20:38,620
That would be great, ma’am.

343
00:20:41,160 --> 00:20:43,220
It seems we’re not getting anywhere.

344
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:47,300
A good student like you…

345
00:20:47,970 --> 00:20:50,300
wouldn’t want me
to call your parents in, would you?

346
00:20:56,520 --> 00:20:57,490
Go ahead and call them.

347
00:20:59,700 --> 00:21:00,900
Actually,…

348
00:21:02,220 --> 00:21:04,500
my dad wants to have a word with you, too.

349
00:21:12,680 --> 00:21:14,890
DON’T LET TYLER EAT ANYTHING

350
00:21:14,910 --> 00:21:15,800
Wow.

351
00:21:16,630 --> 00:21:19,000
Ji’s part looks flawless.

352
00:21:19,740 --> 00:21:20,780
It’s not that good.

353
00:21:20,990 --> 00:21:22,030
And Ty’s part…

354
00:21:22,170 --> 00:21:24,220
How’s that? It’s good, right?

355
00:21:24,370 --> 00:21:26,060
Took me so long to perfect this.

356
00:21:28,460 --> 00:21:29,060
Looks great.

357
00:21:30,190 --> 00:21:32,310
Jiraphat seems like someone
we can count on.

358
00:21:32,340 --> 00:21:33,300
I know, right?

359
00:21:33,570 --> 00:21:36,250
Since Ji arrived at school early
on the first day of the semester,

360
00:21:36,290 --> 00:21:39,600
I could tell
that Jiraphat had changed a lot.

361
00:21:39,800 --> 00:21:43,420
-Please. You can’t be serious.
-Why?

362
00:21:43,560 --> 00:21:46,000
You thought I was late for school,
didn’t you?

363
00:21:46,030 --> 00:21:46,780
See?

364
00:21:46,990 --> 00:21:50,200
The old Jiraphat
wouldn’t talk back like that.

365
00:21:51,820 --> 00:21:52,600
Ouch!

366
00:21:54,570 --> 00:21:55,500
What happened?

367
00:21:56,380 --> 00:21:58,300
Nothing.

368
00:21:58,670 --> 00:21:59,710
Ty!

369
00:22:00,230 --> 00:22:03,000
Finish this
or you don’t get to take a break.

370
00:22:05,240 --> 00:22:06,020
Listen to this.

371
00:22:06,290 --> 00:22:06,780
Yeah.

372
00:22:06,880 --> 00:22:10,700
Yesterday, Lita told me
the president was a psychic.

373
00:22:11,210 --> 00:22:16,200
He asked Lita to investigate something
but she refused.

374
00:22:16,460 --> 00:22:20,500
Then he said,
“Think of it as an early internship.”

375
00:22:22,020 --> 00:22:23,500
How does that make him a psychic?

376
00:22:23,950 --> 00:22:27,100
Everyone knows
Lita wanted to study engineering.

377
00:22:27,410 --> 00:22:30,140
She only changed her mind and decided
to study communications recently.

378
00:22:30,500 --> 00:22:32,620
And I’m the only one who knows.

379
00:22:35,080 --> 00:22:37,940
(I'm so glad you've agreed
to be interviewed on our show.)

380
00:22:37,960 --> 00:22:40,090
(It's been a long time.
How have you been?)

381
00:22:40,120 --> 00:22:41,270
(I'm happy to be here, too.)

382
00:22:41,300 --> 00:22:43,130
(It's been a long time since we last met.)

383
00:22:43,140 --> 00:22:45,180
(It's an honor to be invited here today.)

384
00:22:46,810 --> 00:22:48,180
You're overthinking it.

385
00:22:48,730 --> 00:22:50,470
With how much she knows
about other people's business,

386
00:22:50,510 --> 00:22:52,600
even I could tell
she was meant to be a journalist.

387
00:22:53,040 --> 00:22:56,300
I was more surprised
she said she wanted to study engineering.

388
00:22:57,920 --> 00:22:59,380
Speaking of the president,

389
00:22:59,420 --> 00:23:04,000
I’m thinking about his old nickname
back in middle school: Ruth the Sweet Tooth.

390
00:23:04,930 --> 00:23:06,000
Ruth the Sweet Tooth?

391
00:23:06,500 --> 00:23:07,800
Wow.

392
00:23:08,270 --> 00:23:10,380
Since you threw
the volleyball in his face,

393
00:23:10,420 --> 00:23:12,020
you seem to be interested in him.

394
00:23:13,950 --> 00:23:15,540
No. I’m just curious.

395
00:23:16,770 --> 00:23:17,500
Mmm.

396
00:23:18,190 --> 00:23:19,600
Back in middle school,

397
00:23:19,900 --> 00:23:24,650
Pim, Lita, the president, Tawan, and I
were in the same class.

398
00:23:24,680 --> 00:23:26,250
GRADE 7, CLASS 1

399
00:23:30,600 --> 00:23:32,620
Aren’t you coming down
to the cafeteria for lunch with us?

400
00:23:35,240 --> 00:23:37,740
Wow, those look so good.
Can I have some?

401
00:23:38,790 --> 00:23:39,500
No.

402
00:23:40,170 --> 00:23:41,800
Hey, let me have some.

403
00:23:42,650 --> 00:23:45,000
-Let me try. Let me try.
-No.

404
00:23:45,380 --> 00:23:46,350
No.

405
00:23:48,360 --> 00:23:50,480
After that, he never brought
his lunch box to school again.

406
00:23:50,520 --> 00:23:53,430
But Tawan shouldn't have teased Ruth

407
00:23:53,470 --> 00:23:55,400
just because
he wouldn't share his desserts.

408
00:23:55,920 --> 00:24:00,400
He went from Ruth the Kindhearted
to President Ruth the Stern.

409
00:24:01,500 --> 00:24:02,230
Ouch!

410
00:24:02,540 --> 00:24:03,580
-Stop!
-Stop!

411
00:24:03,780 --> 00:24:05,000
Not the same trick.

412
00:24:05,570 --> 00:24:07,700
You think we would believe you?

413
00:24:07,720 --> 00:24:08,380
Sh*t!
Oh, Ji.

414
00:24:09,400 --> 00:24:11,000
You’re playing along this time?

415
00:24:12,260 --> 00:24:13,300
-Sh*t.
-Sh*t.

416
00:24:15,070 --> 00:24:16,090
Ji, I’m hurt!

417
00:24:16,350 --> 00:24:19,550
If I die, tell my dad I love him.

418
00:24:19,860 --> 00:24:21,380
I love you, Dad.

419
00:24:24,030 --> 00:24:25,900
Ouch! Ow, ow, ow! Please, that hurts.

420
00:24:25,940 --> 00:24:27,380
Please go easy on me.

421
00:24:27,420 --> 00:24:29,000
-That hurts.
-Tyler.

422
00:24:29,500 --> 00:24:31,110
I’ll go back and help our friends.

423
00:24:32,630 --> 00:24:34,870
Oww! That hurts.

424
00:24:34,910 --> 00:24:36,500
Stay still.

425
00:24:36,800 --> 00:24:37,710
Ahh!

426
00:25:14,700 --> 00:25:16,200
Hey, come here.

427
00:25:16,600 --> 00:25:17,660
I have something big.

428
00:25:18,660 --> 00:25:20,760
-Something big?
-Don’t just stand there. Come here.

429
00:25:22,780 --> 00:25:24,800
It’s an emergency.

430
00:25:25,160 --> 00:25:25,900
What is it?

431
00:25:26,310 --> 00:25:27,900
It’s Ms. Suda.

432
00:25:29,540 --> 00:25:34,200
Pim and I went to the bathroom
and Lita told us.

433
00:25:34,420 --> 00:25:36,420
It’s gonna blow your mind.

434
00:25:37,190 --> 00:25:39,220
Ms. Suda has been reported.

435
00:25:41,510 --> 00:25:42,100
That’s it?

436
00:25:42,200 --> 00:25:43,580
Not just that.

437
00:25:43,620 --> 00:25:45,420
She was also called
to the principal’s office.

438
00:25:46,180 --> 00:25:47,700
The principal knows about this, too?

439
00:25:48,180 --> 00:25:49,700
I told you it was huge.

440
00:25:50,270 --> 00:25:52,400
The point isn’t that someone
filed a complaint.

441
00:25:52,760 --> 00:25:55,860
It’s who filed it.

442
00:26:02,280 --> 00:26:05,900
The trays for Grades 10 and 12
have been brought into the auditorium.

443
00:26:06,340 --> 00:26:08,740
Only the Grade 11 trays
are still incomplete.

444
00:26:10,660 --> 00:26:11,620
About Ms. Suda…

445
00:26:12,060 --> 00:26:14,540
I thought you and your dad
don’t get along.

446
00:26:18,460 --> 00:26:22,820
You said, “I’d rather die
than asking him for help.”

447
00:26:23,870 --> 00:26:26,370
We’re not that incompetent.

448
00:26:27,200 --> 00:26:30,380
It might take some time
but we’ll finally find a way.

449
00:26:30,710 --> 00:26:32,800
You didn’t have to hurry
and ask for his help.

450
00:26:41,220 --> 00:26:45,600
(I remember that after the student council
filed a complaint,)

451
00:26:45,990 --> 00:26:48,900
(Ruth, the class president,
was summoned by Ms. Suda.)

452
00:26:49,370 --> 00:26:50,260
(But back then,)
STUDENT COUNCIL ROOM

453
00:26:51,240 --> 00:26:52,940
(It ended with her agreeing
to give the notes)

454
00:26:52,980 --> 00:26:56,160
(she’d given the Science-Math students
to the Arts-Languages students, too.)

455
00:26:56,340 --> 00:26:58,500
(But she continued to teach
in the same biased way.)

456
00:26:59,310 --> 00:27:02,700
(That’s why I was surprised
the principal had known about it.)

457
00:27:23,310 --> 00:27:25,200
How’s your face after getting hit?

458
00:27:25,260 --> 00:27:26,100
Sorry about that.

459
00:27:30,440 --> 00:27:31,400
It’s fine now.

460
00:27:39,890 --> 00:27:41,100
About Ms. Suda…

461
00:27:44,880 --> 00:27:47,350
What about her?

462
00:27:48,990 --> 00:27:50,100
Don’t worry.

463
00:27:50,710 --> 00:27:51,900
I’ve talked to her.

464
00:27:52,680 --> 00:27:55,380
You can attend class as usual.

465
00:27:56,670 --> 00:27:57,860
Why would you go this far for me?

466
00:27:58,170 --> 00:28:00,700
You disagree with what I did?

467
00:28:03,300 --> 00:28:04,470
It’s not that.

468
00:28:04,970 --> 00:28:06,540
Based on the attendance records,

469
00:28:06,970 --> 00:28:09,740
Arts-Languages students
seem to skip class more often

470
00:28:09,780 --> 00:28:11,490
on days when they have math class.

471
00:28:12,340 --> 00:28:14,690
If I let this go,
it definitely won’t end here.

472
00:28:16,350 --> 00:28:18,870
But Ms. Suda hasn’t caused
any big trouble this semester.

473
00:28:18,900 --> 00:28:19,740
Jiraphat

474
00:28:23,920 --> 00:28:27,340
The fact that she abandoned your class
and refused to continue teaching

475
00:28:27,990 --> 00:28:30,100
was a serious dereliction
of her duty as a teacher.

476
00:28:36,050 --> 00:28:37,600
When she called me in,

477
00:28:39,260 --> 00:28:42,900
she said your class
wasn’t as good academically as mine,

478
00:28:43,310 --> 00:28:45,600
so what she did was justified.

479
00:28:47,460 --> 00:28:48,460
But it wasn’t.

480
00:28:50,750 --> 00:28:52,900
Just because your class
isn’t as good academically as mine

481
00:28:53,190 --> 00:28:57,260
doesn’t mean she can treat you
however she wants.

482
00:29:01,080 --> 00:29:06,130
A teacher can’t just abandon
their students like that.

483
00:29:08,890 --> 00:29:10,400
As student council president,

484
00:29:12,670 --> 00:29:14,400
I can’t let this continue.

485
00:29:29,280 --> 00:29:32,170
TEACHER APPRECIATION CEREMONY

486
00:29:50,660 --> 00:29:51,650
Thank you.

487
00:29:52,310 --> 00:29:53,300
Thank you so much.

488
00:30:16,120 --> 00:30:20,700
Hey, I heard Ms. Suda
received a warning letter.

489
00:30:21,050 --> 00:30:24,340
She’ll probably have to stop teaching
until the investigation is complete.

490
00:30:25,210 --> 00:30:27,700
Then who’s going to teach us math?

491
00:30:27,960 --> 00:30:29,400
I heard it’s going to be a new teacher.

492
00:30:29,590 --> 00:30:32,600
They’ll probably start
after we get back from the field trip.

493
00:30:34,600 --> 00:30:35,820
Where should we sit?

494
00:30:36,570 --> 00:30:39,000
Hey, hey, that’s the second piece.

495
00:30:39,820 --> 00:30:40,630
Over there?

496
00:30:41,310 --> 00:30:42,200
Yeah, over there?

497
00:30:42,390 --> 00:30:43,900
Have some water.

498
00:30:44,670 --> 00:30:45,550
You want some water?

499
00:30:45,580 --> 00:30:46,680
It’s okay.

500
00:30:46,710 --> 00:30:48,000
Can I sit?

501
00:30:52,970 --> 00:30:54,790
The three core members
of the student council.

502
00:30:54,820 --> 00:30:56,200
Any updates on Ms. Suda?

503
00:30:56,510 --> 00:30:58,170
She was called in by the principal,

504
00:30:58,210 --> 00:31:00,100
so the investigation
will probably take a while.

505
00:31:00,650 --> 00:31:06,500
I heard you let the offering tray cut you
so people would feel sorry for you.

506
00:31:06,830 --> 00:31:10,300
Class 7 always has something
unexpected going on.

507
00:31:10,490 --> 00:31:12,660
Tawan, do you think
being on the student council

508
00:31:12,680 --> 00:31:13,940
means you can say whatever you want?

509
00:31:14,110 --> 00:31:16,220
Hey, I was complimenting you.

510
00:31:16,580 --> 00:31:17,370
But at least

511
00:31:17,410 --> 00:31:19,600
our class’s fruit offering tray
tastes good.

512
00:31:20,600 --> 00:31:22,400
-Hey.
-Hey, Kongpob!

513
00:31:23,650 --> 00:31:25,100
My fried chicken.

514
00:31:25,140 --> 00:31:26,780
Hey, my fried chicken.

515
00:31:27,810 --> 00:31:28,700
Yummy.

516
00:31:29,730 --> 00:31:31,600
You have two pieces.

517
00:31:31,970 --> 00:31:36,320
-He was afraid you would be too full.
-Tawan, I only had two pieces.

518
00:31:51,520 --> 00:31:52,300
Damn.

519
00:31:52,960 --> 00:31:54,000
So heavy.

520
00:31:58,220 --> 00:32:02,220
Jean and I have told you many times
that this bench is hard and uncomfortable.

521
00:32:02,670 --> 00:32:06,500
But when Tyler and Kongpob complained,
you immediately bought a new couch.

522
00:32:06,800 --> 00:32:12,420
Because your mom was afraid
Ty and Kong wouldn’t come over anymore.

523
00:32:15,150 --> 00:32:19,400
You haven’t forgotten
our annual health checkup, right, Dad?

524
00:32:19,480 --> 00:32:22,130
Your mom and I have already scheduled it.
We’ll go together.

525
00:32:27,390 --> 00:32:31,340
Dad, I have a school trip
at the end of this month.

526
00:32:31,560 --> 00:32:32,580
It’s overnight, too.

527
00:32:33,690 --> 00:32:34,620
Huh?

528
00:32:35,260 --> 00:32:36,800
Why are you making that face, Dad?

529
00:32:37,060 --> 00:32:38,700
I’m just going on a school trip.

530
00:32:38,860 --> 00:32:42,420
Usually, whenever the school
has an activity,

531
00:32:42,450 --> 00:32:45,260
you pretend to be sick
so you can stay home.

532
00:32:46,950 --> 00:32:48,260
But this is a good thing.

533
00:32:48,730 --> 00:32:52,010
Your school years are the perfect time
to gain new experiences

534
00:32:52,040 --> 00:32:53,800
and make good memories,

535
00:32:54,430 --> 00:32:56,300
so you won’t have any regrets
later in life.

536
00:32:59,490 --> 00:33:00,300
Yes, Dad.

537
00:33:00,580 --> 00:33:01,420
That’s good.

538
00:33:05,400 --> 00:33:07,300
Why are you carrying so much stuff today?

539
00:33:07,340 --> 00:33:09,050
We have art class today,

540
00:33:09,090 --> 00:33:10,800
and the teacher asked us
to bring our own supplies.

541
00:33:11,350 --> 00:33:13,000
Hurry up, or you’ll get stuck in traffic.

542
00:33:13,120 --> 00:33:13,780
Okay, Dad.

543
00:33:15,450 --> 00:33:18,500
Here, take this new handkerchief.

544
00:33:19,050 --> 00:33:22,100
Use it to wipe your art supplies
so they don’t stain your bag.

545
00:33:22,330 --> 00:33:24,700
-Okay, mom. I’ll go then.
-Okay.

546
00:33:31,350 --> 00:33:33,700
Can you guess what this is?

547
00:33:35,150 --> 00:33:35,860
A cat.
Nope.

548
00:33:36,740 --> 00:33:37,500
A bear.

549
00:33:38,260 --> 00:33:41,000
How is that a bear?
This is definitely a cat.

550
00:33:41,090 --> 00:33:42,220
It’s Bear, Ji.

551
00:33:44,070 --> 00:33:45,940
Oh, my cat, Bear.

552
00:33:49,170 --> 00:33:50,100
This is cute.

553
00:33:51,440 --> 00:33:52,600
Of course.

554
00:33:52,760 --> 00:33:55,470
My dad is a famous artist,
and he taught me himself.

555
00:33:55,890 --> 00:33:57,500
Isn’t your dad an engineer?

556
00:33:57,820 --> 00:33:59,660
How did he become a painter?

557
00:34:00,290 --> 00:34:02,220
My dad can do anything.

558
00:34:05,150 --> 00:34:08,000
Why haven’t you started drawing yet?

559
00:34:08,040 --> 00:34:09,340
It’s already the second period.

560
00:34:10,060 --> 00:34:11,320
There’s no need to rush.

561
00:34:11,350 --> 00:34:13,100
The teacher never said
we had to turn it in class.

562
00:34:19,130 --> 00:34:21,000
I think we should find a new spot.

563
00:34:21,540 --> 00:34:23,900
Why, Ji? It’s fun right here.

564
00:34:24,630 --> 00:34:27,100
How is this fun?
Don’t you see those people?

565
00:34:27,260 --> 00:34:28,840
If a volleyball…

566
00:34:31,070 --> 00:34:32,380
Ji, you okay?

567
00:34:32,590 --> 00:34:33,700
Let’s go to the medical room.

568
00:34:34,620 --> 00:34:36,490
Sorry. Are you okay?

569
00:34:37,450 --> 00:34:39,500
Oh, Ji and Ty?

570
00:34:40,000 --> 00:34:41,900
You must be fine then.

571
00:34:42,010 --> 00:34:45,030
Hey, why would you say that?

572
00:34:45,070 --> 00:34:48,670
Didn’t you throw a volleyball
at the president’s face?

573
00:34:48,940 --> 00:34:50,000
Let’s call this even.

574
00:34:50,970 --> 00:34:52,300
Where’s Ruth?

575
00:34:53,850 --> 00:34:54,800
What the hell?

576
00:34:55,080 --> 00:34:58,340
The cute vice president’s here
and you’re asking for Ruth?

577
00:34:58,470 --> 00:35:00,460
Of course he’s asking about someone
who’s not here.

578
00:35:00,630 --> 00:35:03,780
Why would he ask about you?

579
00:35:04,230 --> 00:35:05,300
You’re ganging up on me.

580
00:35:06,720 --> 00:35:07,930
The president will be fine.

581
00:35:08,100 --> 00:35:09,940
He was just called
in to discuss some competition

582
00:35:09,960 --> 00:35:11,100
in the Science Department.

583
00:35:13,140 --> 00:35:14,100
Again?

584
00:35:14,790 --> 00:35:17,700
You seem to be more upset than he is.

585
00:35:18,700 --> 00:35:19,700
But I get it.

586
00:35:19,870 --> 00:35:23,300
We’ve never really been able to enjoy
PE class with him, either.

587
00:35:23,670 --> 00:35:26,000
It’s supposed to be
our only chance to relax,

588
00:35:26,600 --> 00:35:27,940
yet he keeps getting called away.

589
00:35:30,860 --> 00:35:34,300
This is just like a sitcom
I used to watch.

590
00:35:34,650 --> 00:35:35,660
What sitcom?

591
00:35:36,170 --> 00:35:38,060
Those characters who have the same name.

592
00:35:38,250 --> 00:35:39,100
Mr. Ruth.

593
00:35:39,810 --> 00:35:40,700
Security Ruth.

594
00:35:41,030 --> 00:35:41,700
Auntie Ruth.

595
00:35:42,320 --> 00:35:44,740
And this is… President Ruth.

596
00:35:44,850 --> 00:35:45,660
My friend Ruth.

597
00:35:45,930 --> 00:35:47,000
Student Ruth.

598
00:35:47,340 --> 00:35:49,820
Hey, Wan. What’s taking you so long?

599
00:35:50,000 --> 00:35:53,100
If you were in such a hurry,
why didn’t you come get it yourself?

600
00:35:53,820 --> 00:35:54,600
Ugh.

601
00:35:55,290 --> 00:35:56,200
See you.

602
00:35:59,290 --> 00:36:00,800
Hey, Ji. Where are you going?

603
00:36:00,980 --> 00:36:01,680
Away from here.

604
00:36:01,720 --> 00:36:04,190
Do you want to wait for another volleyball
to hit your face?

605
00:36:05,310 --> 00:36:06,200
What the hell?

606
00:36:07,670 --> 00:36:09,100
Why are you so afraid of a volleyball?

607
00:36:09,360 --> 00:36:10,070
Ouch!

608
00:36:11,350 --> 00:36:12,200
Okay, I give up.

609
00:36:18,460 --> 00:36:20,000
Don’t you want to sit there anymore?

610
00:36:20,260 --> 00:36:22,180
No, I don’t want to get injured.

611
00:36:26,650 --> 00:36:28,770
It’s you and Ty, see? Can you tell?

612
00:36:29,140 --> 00:36:29,900
Of course.

613
00:36:30,380 --> 00:36:31,900
Wow, this is beautiful.

614
00:36:32,450 --> 00:36:35,460
Really? You’re not just saying that?

615
00:36:35,650 --> 00:36:37,100
Why would I do that?

616
00:36:37,650 --> 00:36:39,160
You drew this well in just one hour.

617
00:36:39,190 --> 00:36:41,100
If you told me you were entering
an art competition, I’d believe you.

618
00:36:44,170 --> 00:36:46,300
The teacher has asked me
to enter competitions before.

619
00:36:46,760 --> 00:36:50,000
But if I entered one,
I’d have to stay after school to practice.

620
00:36:51,580 --> 00:36:53,010
I have tutoring classes to go to,

621
00:36:53,050 --> 00:36:54,200
so I don’t have time.

622
00:36:57,930 --> 00:37:01,380
You’re good at social studies anyway.

623
00:37:02,060 --> 00:37:03,460
You’ll get into university for sure.

624
00:37:05,670 --> 00:37:07,710
Do you want to be a fortune-teller
like the president,

625
00:37:07,740 --> 00:37:09,500
the way Lita told us?

626
00:37:11,710 --> 00:37:12,690
No, that’s not it.

627
00:37:15,450 --> 00:37:17,700
Oww, Ji, Pim, can I sit with you guys?

628
00:37:17,850 --> 00:37:19,800
I’ve been hit by a volleyball many times.

629
00:37:20,930 --> 00:37:22,700
There goes my peaceful moment.

630
00:37:22,990 --> 00:37:24,800
Oh, please, Pim.

631
00:37:25,130 --> 00:37:27,650
Ked is at a basketball competition today,
so I was worried you’d be lonely.

632
00:37:30,090 --> 00:37:31,500
My head hurts.

633
00:37:35,670 --> 00:37:37,210
-How’s this?
-There’s a cat, too.

634
00:37:37,250 --> 00:37:37,900
A bear.

635
00:37:38,150 --> 00:37:39,900
-Oh, a bear?
-Little bear.

636
00:37:40,000 --> 00:37:41,000
How is that a bear?

637
00:37:41,250 --> 00:37:42,400
His name is Bear.

638
00:37:44,430 --> 00:37:45,700
Go ahead and grab it, sweetie.

639
00:37:46,800 --> 00:37:47,900
-I’ll have…
-I’ll have…

640
00:37:50,630 --> 00:37:51,500
You first.

641
00:37:55,050 --> 00:37:56,020
A water, please.

642
00:37:58,970 --> 00:38:00,140
Here's your water.

643
00:38:01,250 --> 00:38:03,900
Can I have a cube of ice?

644
00:38:04,220 --> 00:38:07,800
I got hit by a volleyball,
and I think my head’s starting to swell.

645
00:38:09,200 --> 00:38:10,050
Thank you.

646
00:38:12,100 --> 00:38:13,100
It kind of hurts.

647
00:38:14,830 --> 00:38:15,600
What?

648
00:38:17,360 --> 00:38:19,000
I was hit by a volleyball.

649
00:38:19,480 --> 00:38:20,600
It kind of hurt.

650
00:38:28,350 --> 00:38:29,250
Thank you.

651
00:38:46,470 --> 00:38:47,450
Wrong side.

652
00:39:00,430 --> 00:39:02,270
Didn’t you come to get a water?

653
00:39:02,310 --> 00:39:04,100
Here’s your water.

654
00:39:04,590 --> 00:39:05,400
Here.

655
00:39:06,110 --> 00:39:08,030
Thank you. I’ll do it myself.

656
00:39:15,250 --> 00:39:16,800
I’ve never been here.

657
00:39:17,560 --> 00:39:20,420
I didn’t know there’s a haunted house
in our school.

658
00:39:20,870 --> 00:39:23,130
It’s a men’s room not a haunted house.

659
00:39:27,360 --> 00:39:29,000
I’ll wash the rest.

660
00:39:29,280 --> 00:39:30,500
Please just stand there.

661
00:39:30,830 --> 00:39:32,980
Hey, I can wash it properly.

662
00:39:33,380 --> 00:39:35,500
Don’t you see how dirty that brush is?

663
00:39:36,030 --> 00:39:37,400
But you’re super clean.

664
00:39:37,570 --> 00:39:38,900
You don’t have a paint stain on you.

665
00:39:38,940 --> 00:39:41,000
I’m not that bad, look.

666
00:39:41,710 --> 00:39:43,980
Actually, it’s kind of…

667
00:39:45,120 --> 00:39:46,050
Hey, Tawan.

668
00:39:46,740 --> 00:39:47,500
Hey.

669
00:39:48,060 --> 00:39:50,060
You use this bathroom, too?

670
00:39:50,210 --> 00:39:51,740
I’ve never seen you here.

671
00:39:52,110 --> 00:39:54,220
Hey, why can we use it, too?

672
00:39:54,540 --> 00:39:56,800
Is it your personal pee spot?

673
00:39:56,990 --> 00:39:58,700
Hey, I’m not a dog.

674
00:39:58,990 --> 00:40:01,100
Hold on. I’ll be back for you.

675
00:40:05,570 --> 00:40:06,300
Hey.

676
00:40:08,080 --> 00:40:08,800
Hey.

677
00:40:11,280 --> 00:40:11,980
Ji

678
00:40:12,230 --> 00:40:13,500
Water Dragon!

679
00:40:14,640 --> 00:40:15,500
Tyler!

680
00:40:15,610 --> 00:40:17,500
How was my move?

681
00:40:18,640 --> 00:40:20,060
What are you doing? Can I join?

682
00:40:26,660 --> 00:40:29,380
Tawan, my friend. Why did you hurt me?

683
00:40:29,560 --> 00:40:30,940
You’re my enemy.

684
00:40:31,510 --> 00:40:33,820
Bring it on if you dare!

685
00:40:36,230 --> 00:40:37,100
Water Dragon!

686
00:40:37,130 --> 00:40:38,200
Boom, boom, boom.

687
00:40:38,820 --> 00:40:40,400
Hey, hey, hey.

688
00:40:41,670 --> 00:40:43,200
They hell are you doing?

689
00:40:44,240 --> 00:40:46,200
The Martial Arts Tournament.

690
00:40:46,780 --> 00:40:49,340
Would you be interested in joining?

691
00:40:49,550 --> 00:40:51,990
Indeed. I’ve long wished
to test my skills against you.

692
00:40:52,020 --> 00:40:53,670
Show me what you got.

693
00:41:03,560 --> 00:41:05,020
Want to borrow my handkerchief?

694
00:41:07,540 --> 00:41:10,420
Ji, this is a fight
between class 1 and class 7.

695
00:41:10,590 --> 00:41:12,420
Why are you helping an enemy?

696
00:41:14,600 --> 00:41:15,400
Hold this.

697
00:41:16,490 --> 00:41:17,200
Ha!

698
00:41:17,570 --> 00:41:18,700
-Come on
-Yes!

699
00:41:18,990 --> 00:41:21,220
Get out of the way. The big guy’s here!

700
00:41:28,300 --> 00:41:29,800
Water Dragon!

701
00:41:30,300 --> 00:41:32,400
Here comes The Ass Whooper!

702
00:41:34,780 --> 00:41:35,860
Water Dragon!

703
00:41:42,900 --> 00:41:44,060
I can’t believe this.

704
00:41:44,350 --> 00:41:47,460
When I heard that Class 1 and Class 7
had fought in the old bathroom,

705
00:41:47,700 --> 00:41:49,000
I was shocked.

706
00:41:49,590 --> 00:41:52,800
How could you two
get into a fight with anyone?

707
00:41:53,600 --> 00:41:54,300
Now what?

708
00:41:54,430 --> 00:41:55,890
Do you have to go to the Hell Room again?

709
00:41:56,090 --> 00:41:58,650
Mr. Narong figured
we hadn’t caused any serious trouble,

710
00:41:58,690 --> 00:42:01,460
so he just made us mop the floor
and wipe the sinks.

711
00:42:01,500 --> 00:42:02,600
What’s wrong with you?

712
00:42:02,730 --> 00:42:04,800
You’re going to the beach tomorrow.

713
00:42:05,030 --> 00:42:06,260
Why did you do that?

714
00:42:11,700 --> 00:42:12,380
Tyler.

715
00:42:13,180 --> 00:42:14,800
Have you seen my paintbrush cloth?

716
00:42:16,330 --> 00:42:18,800
No. Did you drop it somewhere?

717
00:42:21,580 --> 00:42:23,050
Hmm, I guess so.

718
00:42:32,740 --> 00:42:34,060
What’s that cloth?

719
00:42:36,140 --> 00:42:36,900
Oh.

720
00:42:37,760 --> 00:42:38,900
Someone dropped it.

721
00:42:39,330 --> 00:42:40,450
Give it to me then.

722
00:42:40,540 --> 00:42:43,300
I’ll put it in the lost-and-found box.

723
00:42:45,070 --> 00:42:47,400
Oh, no, I mean…

724
00:42:48,280 --> 00:42:50,000
Someone gave it to me.

725
00:42:59,920 --> 00:43:01,180
Do you wanna wipe your face?

726
00:43:03,470 --> 00:43:05,550
My mom gave it to me
to clean my paintbrushes,

727
00:43:05,590 --> 00:43:06,640
but I haven’t used it.

728
00:43:06,680 --> 00:43:08,100
I promise it’s clean.

729
00:43:10,510 --> 00:43:11,400
Thanks.

730
00:44:19,890 --> 00:44:21,570
Would you be interested in joining
the student council?

731
00:44:21,890 --> 00:44:23,060
I’ll have to pass.

732
00:44:23,460 --> 00:44:24,970
Won’t you regret it
for the rest of your life?

733
00:44:27,970 --> 00:44:29,620
(When you keep sneaking glances
at someone,)

734
00:44:29,660 --> 00:44:32,470
(unless they admire them
or have a crush on them,)

735
00:44:33,840 --> 00:44:35,220
(there’s usually no reason for it.)

736
00:44:35,320 --> 00:44:38,060
If only they’re looking out of interest,

737
00:44:38,420 --> 00:44:39,500
but…

738
00:44:40,860 --> 00:44:45,140
Talking to you makes me happier
than watching the sunset.
